.docname {mise}
.include {docs}

.glossary
    | Word | Meaning | Example |
    |---|---|---|
    | Pin | One exact version written in a file | `python = "3.14.8"` |
    | `PATH` | The list of folders the shell searches for a program | `/usr/bin`, `~/.cargo/bin` |
    | Ambient tool | A tool found on the machine's own `PATH`, installed by someone else | A Node from `nvm` |
    | Activation | A shell hook that puts the pinned tools on `PATH` whenever the shell is inside the repo | `mise activate bash` |
    | `mise exec` | Run one command with the pinned tools first on `PATH` | `mise exec -- cargo test` |
    | Backend | Where `mise` downloads a tool from | `ubi:moonrepo/moon` is a GitHub release |

`mise` installs every language and CLI CrowdVision uses, at the version pinned in `.mise.toml`, and puts exactly those versions on `PATH` for a command. One file answers "which version?" for every machine and for CI.

---

## The pins

```toml
[tools]
node = "24"
python = "3.14.8"
rust = "1.99.1"
go = "1.27.3"
"ubi:moonrepo/moon" = "2.6.4"
uv = "0.13.5"
istioctl = "1.31.3"

[env]
QUARKDOWN_VERSION = "2.7.0"
```

| Pin | Used for | Also read by |
|---|---|---|
| `node = "24"` | `frontend`, `sensor-simulator`, `eslint-config` | Only the major is pinned: `mise` takes the newest 24, today 24.21.0 |
| `python = "3.14.8"` | `agent`, simulators, `ap-collector`, `scripts/*.py` | CI reads `.python-version` instead |
| `rust = "1.99.1"` | The six Rust services and four schema crates | CI's `setup-rust` action; `cd-registry.yml` builds the Rust base image with it |
| `go = "1.27.3"` | The four Go services and three Go libraries | CI's `setup-go` action; the `golang` tag in the four Go `Dockerfile`s must match by hand |
| `ubi:moonrepo/moon` | `moon` itself, from its GitHub release | |
| `uv` | Python packages and virtual environments | |
| `istioctl` | `just k8s istio-install` | |
| `QUARKDOWN_VERSION` | Not a tool: an environment variable | `just docs build` and the docs workflows compare the installed Quarkdown to it |

Quarkdown is not a `mise` tool. Its release is a 92 MB zip with its own Java runtime, and the `mise` backends keep only the launcher script, which then cannot find its classes. So it is installed separately, and only its version lives here.

---

## How a command gets its tools

Every `just` recipe that calls a pinned tool starts with `mise exec --`. Here is what that does for `mise exec -- cargo --version`:

.mermaid
    sequenceDiagram
        autonumber
        participant S as shell
        participant Mi as mise
        participant F as mise.toml
        participant St as mise store
        participant C as cargo
        S->>Mi: mise exec -- cargo --version
        Mi->>F: read the tools section
        F-->>Mi: rust 1.99.1, go 1.27.3, node 24, ...
        Mi->>St: is each version installed?
        alt missing
            Mi->>St: download and install it first
        end
        Mi->>Mi: new PATH with each tool's folder first
        Mi->>Mi: set RUSTUP_TOOLCHAIN=1.99.1 and similar
        Mi->>C: start cargo with that environment
        C-->>S: cargo 1.99.1
        Note over C: every child of cargo inherits the same environment

| Step | What happens | Real value on a developer machine |
|---|---|---|
| 2–3 | `mise` finds `.mise.toml` by walking up from the current folder | `/home/…/crowd-vision/.mise.toml` |
| 4 | Each tool sits in its own folder in the store | `~/.local/share/mise/installs/rust/1.99.1` |
| 6 | Those folders go first on `PATH`, ahead of anything installed by hand | |
| 7 | Rust is handed to `rustup` through an environment variable | `RUSTUP_TOOLCHAIN=1.99.1` |
| 8–9 | The command runs | `cargo 1.99.1` |
| note | Children inherit it: `moon`, then `cargo`, then build scripts | `mise exec -- moon --version` gives `moon 2.6.4` |

**Example.** A laptop has Rust 1.85 from `rustup` as its default.

- `cargo build` in a plain shell uses 1.85. A newer `clippy` lint present in CI does not fire.
- `mise exec -- cargo build` uses 1.99.1, the same as CI.
- `which cargo` can still print `~/.cargo/bin/cargo` under `mise exec`: that is `rustup`'s launcher, and `RUSTUP_TOOLCHAIN` picks the version behind it.

---

## Two ways to get the tools

| Way | When | How |
|---|---|---|
| `mise exec --` | Recipes, scripts, anywhere a shell setup cannot be assumed | Wraps one command |
| Activation | Typing commands by hand | `mise activate bash`, `zsh`, `fish` or `pwsh` in the shell profile |

Activation is optional. A fresh clone with only `just` and `mise` installed runs every recipe, because each one wraps its tool.

.mermaid
    flowchart TD
        A["a command needs a tool"] --> B{"run by a just recipe?"}
        B -- yes --> C["recipe already uses mise exec"]
        B -- no --> D{"shell has mise activated?"}
        D -- yes --> E["pinned tool on PATH"]
        D -- no --> F["ambient tool, maybe the wrong version, maybe missing"]
        F --> G["prefix with mise exec --"]

---

## Who uses mise, and who does not

| Caller | Uses `mise` | How it gets versions |
|---|---|---|
| Every `just` recipe calling `moon`, `uv`, `python3`, `npx`, `istioctl` or a script | Yes | `mise exec --` |
| `moon` and every task it runs | Yes, through its parent | Inherited `PATH` |
| `docker`, `k3d`, `kubectl`, `quarkdown` | No | Installed by hand; not managed |
| CI per-language workflows | No | `setup-rust` and `setup-go` read `.mise.toml` with `grep`; `setup-node` asks for `24.x`; `setup-python` reads `.python-version` |
| `ci-acceptance.yml` | Yes | `jdx/mise-action` |
| Docker images | No | Their own base image tags, kept equal to the pins by hand |

---

## Changing a version

A version bump is its own commit, because CI reads the same numbers and lint rules can change with them.

1. Edit `.mise.toml`.
2. `mise install`.
3. For Go: change the `golang` tag in the four Go `Dockerfile`s too.
4. For Python: change `.python-version` too.
5. `just lint all` and `just test all`: a new `clippy` or `go vet` can fail code that passed before.

---

## Traps

| Symptom | Cause | Fix |
|---|---|---|
| `uv: command not found` | A command ran outside `mise` | `mise exec -- uv …`, or activate `mise` |
| Local lint passes, CI fails | A different Rust or Go ran locally | Run through `just` or `mise exec` |
| `mise WARN mise version … available` | `mise` itself is older than the latest | Harmless; `mise self-update` |
| `link.exe not found` (Windows) | Rust needs the MSVC linker | Install Visual Studio Build Tools, "Desktop development with C++" |
| Linker errors on Linux or macOS | No C compiler | `build-essential`, or `xcode-select --install` |
| `quarkdown … installed, but .mise.toml pins …` | Quarkdown version differs from `QUARKDOWN_VERSION` | Install the pinned build |

Setup steps for a new machine: [Setting Up the Environment](../setup/setting-up.qd).
